      "html": "<p>Your van checks run on paper that vanishes behind the seat, or an app nobody opens. Both leave a hole in your record.</p><p>This guide compares paper, iAuditor and WhatsApp.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>The DVSA guide says a walkaround check must be done every day for the commercial vehicles it covers. For vans, check what applies to your fleet.</li><li>Paper is valid, but it gets lost. Gaps look bad.</li><li>iAuditor (made by SafetyCulture, now called Mitti) suits firms with a rollout plan and someone who owns it.</li><li>Quickler runs the check in WhatsApp. No new app. No login.</li><li>The DVSA guide says safety inspection records and driver defect reports must be kept for at least 15 months. Digital records cost nothing to keep.</li><li>Ask yourself: could you show DVSA three months of checks in ten minutes?</li></ul>"
    },
    {
