      "eyebrow": "What DVSA looks for",
      "heading": "Three things, then the ten-minute test",
      "html": "<p>The DVSA guide says examiners assess how effective an operator's system is, and electronic records must be available for examination. Three records are worth having.</p><p><strong>1. Checks done.</strong> A record for each vehicle each day, with the driver's name and the time. A blank logbook for a day the driver was elsewhere is not proof.</p><p><strong>2. Defects reported.</strong> What was found, who found it and when.</p><p><strong>3. Proof you acted.</strong> A defect with no follow-up is the worst record to show. It proves you knew.</p><p>The 15-month rule in the DVSA guide is written for the commercial vehicles it covers. If you run vans, keeping the same 15 months is the safe choice.</p><p>The test: if DVSA asked today for the last three months across every vehicle, could you produce it in ten minutes?</p>"
    },
