      "html": "<p>The DVSA guide to maintaining roadworthiness (updated 28 April 2025) says a minimum of one walkaround check must be carried out every day, that records of safety inspections and driver defect reports must be kept for at least 15 months, and that examiners assess how effective an operator's system is. It covers commercial goods and passenger carrying vehicles and does not name vans as a separate case, so we removed the claims that vans have a 3 month window or that DVSA asks van fleets for a rolling sample. Section 40A of the Road Traffic Act 1988 is the offence of using a vehicle in a dangerous condition (Great Britain). Competitor lines are out of scope. Official sources checked on 18 November 2026.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/guide-to-maintaining-roadworthiness/guide-to-maintaining-roadworthiness-commercial-goods-and-passenger-carrying-vehicles\" rel=\"noopener\">Guide to maintaining roadworthiness (DVSA, gov.uk)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1988/52/section/40A\" rel=\"noopener\">Road Traffic Act 1988 section 40A (legislation.gov.uk)</a></li></ul>"
